<BaseLayout title="KI1 (Yongquan) — Gushing Spring Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate KI1 (Yongquan) — the Gushing Spring point on the sole of the foot. Benefits include calming the mind, grounding energy, and insomnia relief." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "KI1 (Yongquan)", href: "/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point" }]} articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">KI1 (Yongquan) — Gushing Spring</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">KI1, known as Yongquan or Gushing Spring, is the first point of the Kidney meridian. Located on the sole of the foot, it is powerfully grounding, calming, and used for insomnia and anxiety.</p>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/ki1-location.webp" alt="KI1 acupressure point location on the sole of the foot" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" />
    <p class="mb-3">KI1 is on the sole of the foot, in the depression that forms when the foot is pointed, approximately one-third of the distance from the base of the toes toward the heel, between the second and third metatarsal bones.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Calming and grounding:</strong> KI1 draws energy downward, making it excellent for anxiety, restlessness, and feeling ungrounded.</li>
      <li><strong>Insomnia:</strong> It promotes deep sleep by calming the mind and settling the spirit (Shen).</li>
      <li><strong>High blood pressure:</strong> Regular stimulation may help lower blood pressure by promoting relaxation.</li>
      <li><strong>Hot flashes:</strong> KI1 helps cool the body and is used for night sweats and menopausal hot flashes.</li>
      <li><strong>Headaches:</strong> It can relieve headaches, especially those with a sensation of Qi or blood rushing upward.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How to Massage KI1</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit comfortably and place one foot on your opposite thigh.</li>
      <li>Locate the KI1 point on the ball of the foot.</li>
      <li>Use your thumb to apply firm, steady pressure in a circular motion.</li>
      <li>Hold for 1— minutes per foot, breathing deeply.</li>
      <li>Best performed before bed or during stressful moments.</li>
    </ol>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2019 randomized controlled trial in <em>Journal of Cardiovascular Nursing</em> found that KI1 acupressure significantly reduced blood pressure and anxiety in hypertensive patients.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30702572/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Research in <em>Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine</em> showed that KI1 stimulation improved sleep quality and reduced cortisol levels in insomniacs.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29743877/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup></p>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2020 study in <em>Complementary Therapies in Medicine</em> demonstrated that KI1 foot massage reduced hot flash frequency and severity in menopausal women.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32711733/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup></p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Do not apply pressure if you have a foot injury, fracture, or infection on the sole of the foot.</li>
      <li>Avoid if you have diabetic neuropathy or loss of sensation in the feet.</li>
      <li>Use gentle pressure on those with delicate or thin skin on the feet.</li>
      <li>Stop if you feel sharp pain or discomfort.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Related Points</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">KI1 pairs well with <a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point">HT7 (Shenmen)</a> for insomnia and with <a href="/points/kd3-taixi-acupressure-point">KD3 (Taixi)</a> for grounding. For anxiety, combine with <a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point">Yintang</a> and <a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point">PC6 (Neiguan)</a>.</p>
  </section></article>
</BaseLayout>
